   @{" Limits                " link LIMITS}  What does not work (yet)

@endnode

@node INTRO "Introduction"
@{b}Introduction@{ub}

AmiCodeIDE combines a MUI development environment with an AI agent. The agent works like a programmer sitting next to you: it reads files, changes source code, runs the compiler, reads the errors and tries out the program - and asks first when something is risky.

@{b}What the agent can do@{ub}

- read, search, create and change files in a targeted way
- run Shell commands, for example the compiler or a build script
- evaluate compiler errors and improve the code
- search the internet and read web pages (documentation, Aminet)
- handle many languages: C, AmiBlitz3, PureBasic, E, assembler, Lua, Python, ARexx

@{b}Where the intelligence comes from@{ub}

The language model does not run on the Amiga but at a provider on the net (OpenAI, OpenRouter) or on your own computer in the home network (Ollama). AmiCodeIDE sends the conversation there via HTTPS and runs the tools the model calls locally on the Amiga. See @{"Settings" link PREFS}.

@endnode

@node REQUIRE "Requirements"
@{b}Requirements@{ub}

- AmigaOS 3.2 or newer
- 68030 or faster, Fast RAM (16 MB or more recommended)
- MUI 3.8 or newer (tested with MUI 5)
- MUI classes: TextEditor.mcc, NList.mcc, NListview.mcc, NListtree.mcc (required); BetterString.mcc, Busy.mcc and TheBar.mcc are used when present
- TCP/IP stack (bsdsocket.library, for example Roadshow)
- AmiSSL 5 for encrypted connections
- a correctly set system clock (otherwise TLS rejects the certificates)
- access to a language model: an API key from OpenAI or OpenRouter, or an Ollama server in the home network
- at least one compiler or interpreter, see @{"Languages and tools" link TOOLCHAINS}

AmiCodeIDE runs on RTG screens and on the native PAL/NTSC modes; on narrow screens the areas are arranged as register pages.

@endnode

@node INSTALL "Installation"
@{b}Installation@{ub}

Copy these files to a drawer of your choice, for example Work:AmiCodeIDE:

@{code}
   AmiCodeIDE          the program with user interface
   AmiCodeIDE.info     its icon
   amicode             the agent for the Shell (see command line)
   AmiCodeIDE.guide    this manual
   Skills/             Amiga knowledge for the model (see Skills)
   Catalogs/           translations (see Language)
   Help/               translated manuals (see Language)
@{body}

The manual and the Skills drawer must be next to the program: the HELP key looks for the manual there, and without Skills the model lacks the Amiga knowledge (@{"Skills" link SKILLS}).

AmiCodeIDE creates your projects in the drawer Projects next to the program (for example Work:AmiCodeIDE/Projects). It is created on the first start. Set a different place with projects= in the section [ui] of the @{"configuration" link CONFIG}.

On the first start AmiCodeIDE creates its settings in ENVARC:AmiCode/. It remembers the compilers it found in ENVARC:AmiCode/toolchains.conf.

@endnode

@node LANGUAGE "Language"
@{b}Language@{ub}

AmiCodeIDE and amicode are English by default. Other languages come as locale catalogs; a German one is included.

The language follows the Locale settings of your Workbench. With German as preferred language, AmiCodeIDE finds Catalogs/deutsch/AmiCode.catalog next to the program (or in LOCALE:Catalogs/deutsch/) and shows menus, windows and messages in German. The HELP key then opens the German manual from Help/deutsch/.

To choose the language independently of the Locale settings, set an environment variable:

@{code}
   SetEnv SAVE AmiCodeLanguage deutsch     German
   SetEnv SAVE AmiCodeLanguage english     English
   UnSetEnv SAVE AmiCodeLanguage           follow the Locale settings
@{body}

The agent answers in the language you write your tasks in, whatever the catalog.

@endnode

@node FIRSTSTART "First start"
@{b}First start - your first program in five minutes@{ub}

@{b}1. Start@{ub}
Double-click the icon. AmiCodeIDE opens the project used last; on the very first start the project selection or directly the project assistant appears. In the Shell you can also name a project:
@{code}
   Run >NIL: AmiCodeIDE Work:AmiCodeIDE/Projects/myproject
@{body}

@{b}2. Set up access@{ub}
Menu @{i}Project / Settings...@{ui} (Amiga-E). Choose a provider, enter the API key, click @{i}Load models@{ui}, choose a model with a double-click, @{i}Save@{ui}. Details: @{"Settings" link PREFS}.

@{b}3. Create a project@{ub}
Button @{i}New@{ui} in the toolbar (or Amiga-N). Choose name, language and target system and describe what the program should do. With @{i}Start right away@{ui} the agent begins immediately. Details: @{"New project" link NEWPROJECT}.

@{b}4. Watch and take part@{ub}
In the agent area on the right you see what the agent does. When it asks for approval, you decide. With @{i}Build@{ui} and @{i}Run@{ui} in the toolbar you try things yourself. A double-click on an error line in the build log takes you to the right line.

@{b}5. Carry on@{ub}
Just type the next task into the input line: "Add an option for ...", "Why does the program crash when ...?". The agent knows the history so far.

@endnode

@node PREFS "Settings"
@{b}Settings@{ub} (menu Project / Settings..., Amiga-E)

Here you decide which language model the agent uses.

@{b}Providers@{ub}

@{code}
   OpenAI       Models from OpenAI. API key from platform.openai.com
   OpenRouter   Hundreds of models with one key, including Claude,
                Gemini, Qwen, DeepSeek. Key from openrouter.ai
   Ollama       Your own models on a PC in the home network, no key.
                Base URL e.g. http://192.168.1.10:11434/v1
   Ollama Cloud Large open models (Qwen, Kimi, GLM, DeepSeek,
                gpt-oss) on the servers of ollama.com.
                API key from ollama.com/settings/keys
   Custom       Any server with an OpenAI-compatible interface
@{body}

@{b}Fields@{ub}

- @{i}Base URL@{ui}: address of the interface, usually ends in /v1
- @{i}API key@{ui}: the access key; it is shown hidden
- @{i}Model@{ui}: name of the model. With @{i}Load models@{ui} AmiCodeIDE fetches the available models from the provider; a double-click takes one. With OpenAI only chat models, with OpenRouter only models with tool support are offered.
- @{i}Context (tokens)@{ui}: how much conversation the model sees at once. With Ollama the value is passed to the server (default 32768; more needs more video memory). With OpenAI and OpenRouter 0 means "limit of the provider".
- @{i}Reasoning effort@{ui}: how thoroughly a model thinks before answering. Thinking is charged as output (20 dollars per million tokens with Claude Opus). @{i}low@{ui} is enough for most programming tasks and is the default with OpenRouter; @{i}Model's default@{ui} sends nothing. With OpenAI only choose it for models that can reason (gpt-5, o series), others reject the request.
- @{i}Stream answers@{ui}: the text appears while the model is still writing
- @{i}Cost limit per task@{ui}: once a task has cost this many dollars, the agent stops (default 2.00, 0 = no limit). Type "go on" to continue. This only works with providers that report costs - currently OpenRouter.

@{b}When the context fills up@{ub}: when a request reaches 70 percent of the configured context, AmiCodeIDE automatically summarizes the session into a working note and continues with the current task - nothing is lost and the model does not lose track. To start completely fresh use @{i}New session@{ui} or /reset.

@{b}Save, Use, Cancel@{ub}

@{i}Save@{ui} is permanent (ENVARC:), @{i}Use@{ui} only lasts until the Amiga is restarted. On applying, the agent restarts with the new settings; the current conversation continues.

@{b}Which model?@{ub}

Tested with the same task (analog clock with hands, then turned into a MUI application; skills on @{i}Automatic@{ui} except where noted):

@{code}
 moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code   Winner. Loads skills by itself, both
 (OpenRouter)                tasks right at the first attempt, 8-10
                             steps, about $0.02-0.04 per task.
 gpt-5.6-sol (OpenAI)        Loads skills by itself, both tasks
                             right, costs similar to Kimi
                             (estimate - OpenAI does not report
                             costs per request).
 gpt-5.6-luna (OpenAI)       Also successful, more detours than
                             sol, but very cheap (estimate
                             about $0.01 per task).
 qwen3.8:27b (Ollama,        Free, but skills must be set to
 local, free)                "Always" (otherwise it does not load
                             them reliably). Slower, needed one
                             correction (hands mirrored).
@{body}

Not recommended: qwen3-coder (miscounts MUI @{i}End@{ui}/TAG_DONE, goes round in circles), gemma4 (too weak for these tasks), nemotron-3-ultra via Ollama Cloud (does not load the skills and writes a broken MUI dispatcher - crash risk, not started).

@{b}Conclusion@{ub}: moonshotai/kimi-k2.7-code via OpenRouter as the default, qwen3.8:27b locally (with skills=always) as a free alternative. With OpenAI only use models that support tools on /chat/completions - AmiCodeIDE detects and handles the most common special case (reasoning_effort) automatically.

A longer task quickly sends a million tokens - large reasoning models like Claude Opus are correspondingly more expensive than Sonnet or Haiku. @{"/tokens" link COMMANDS} shows the usage.

@{b}Switch for this session only@{ub}: @{"/model" link COMMANDS} in the input line.

The values are stored in ENVARC:AmiCode/amicode.conf, see @{"Configuration" link CONFIG}. This file contains your API keys - do not pass it on.

@endnode

@node APPEARANCE "Appearance"
@{b}Appearance - light, dark, colors and fonts@{ub}

@{b}Switching@{ub}: the cycle gadget at the top right of the main window switches between @{i}Light@{ui} and @{i}Dark@{ui}. The window closes and reopens briefly while the colors change. AmiCodeIDE remembers the choice (ENVARC:AmiCode/theme).

@{b}Settings / Appearance@{ub} (menu Project / Settings..., Amiga-E, tab Appearance):

- @{i}Edit colors for@{ui}: Light and Dark each have their own set of colors. Choose which one you are editing; the switch in the main window decides which one is shown.
- @{i}Areas@{ui}: background and text color for the editor, the agent output, the build log, the lists (project tree, open files), the input lines, the status line and the window with its labels. For buttons, the toolbar and the cycle gadgets only the background can be set - their text stays in the MUI color, so choose a light button color in dark mode.
- @{i}Syntax highlighting and cursor@{ui}: colors for keywords, comments, strings and preprocessor lines in C and assembler files, plus cursor and selection.
- @{i}Default colors@{ui} restores the built-in colors of the set being edited.
- @{i}Fonts@{ui}: a fixed-width font for the editor and the build log and a font for the agent output, lists and input lines, e.g. XEN/11. Empty = the font from the MUI settings. The editor and the agent output use a new font immediately; lists and input lines after restarting AmiCodeIDE.

Each color is chosen with the MUI pen requester: a MUI pen, a color register or any RGB color.

@{b}Limits of MUI 3.8@{ub}: the text of buttons, frame titles and register tabs keeps the look from the MUI settings. Colors apply to the main window; other windows only color their lists.

@endnode

@node NEWPROJECT "New project"
@{b}The project assistant@{ub} (button New, Amiga-N or /new)

The assistant asks for everything the agent needs to start:

- @{b}Name@{ub}: becomes the name of the directory and the program. Letters, digits, _ - and dot are allowed.
- @{b}Will be created in@{ub}: all projects are in the projects drawer Projects next to the program (other place: projects= in the @{"configuration" link CONFIG}). A name may exist only once.
- @{b}Language / tool@{ub}: all compilers and interpreters found on this Amiga, see @{"Languages and tools" link TOOLCHAINS}.
- @{b}Own project (no presets)@{ub}: the agent chooses language and compiler to suit the task, creates the source and build script and enters build and run for the toolbar. For this it needs a description of the task.
- @{b}Program type@{ub}: Shell program, window program (Intuition/GadTools), MUI application or other
- @{b}CPU and system at least@{ub}: the agent writes code that runs on it
- @{b}What should the program do?@{ub}: the more precise, the better, see @{"Working with the agent" link AGENT}
- @{b}Start right away@{ub}: the description goes to the agent as the first task immediately

After @{i}Create@{ui} the project is created with a hello world program, a build script (which sets all required assigns itself), the file AMICODE.md with all details and the project settings. AmiCodeIDE switches to the new project, and with @{i}Start right away@{ui} the agent begins immediately.

@endnode

@node MAINWINDOW "The main window"
@{b}The main window@{ub}

@{code}
 +-----------------------------------------------------------+
 | New Open | Build Run | Save Diff Undo | Stop  New session |
 | Project: ...        Model: ...            Mode: [Project] |
 +-----------+--------------------------+--------------------+
 | Project   | Editor                   | Agent              |
 | tree      |                          |                    |
 |-----------|                          |                    |
 | Open      |                          | [input]    Send    |
 | files     |                          |            Stop    |
 +-----------+--------------------------+--------------------+
 | Build log                                                 |
 | 1> [own command]                             Execute      |
 +-----------------------------------------------------------+
 | Status                         Context 2.5k/32.7k (7%) ... |
 +-----------------------------------------------------------+
@{body}

@{b}Toolbar@{ub}: New (project assistant), Open (another project), Build, Run, Save, Diff, Undo, Stop, New session.

@{b}Open project@{ub} (button Open or Amiga-O): shows all projects in the projects drawer; a double-click opens one. @{i}Other drawer...@{ui} opens any drawer as a project for once. On the next start AmiCodeIDE automatically opens the project used last.
@{i}Build@{ui} recognizes Execute build, make or smake by itself; @{i}Run@{ui} uses the entry run= of the project settings.

@{b}Project tree@{ub}: only the current project - its name at the top, the files below, drawers can be expanded. A double-click opens a file in the editor.

@{b}Open files@{ub}: a click switches the file. A * marks unsaved changes; they are kept when switching. Amiga-W closes the file shown.

@{b}Editor@{ub}: C and assembler source is highlighted (keywords bold, comments italic). Saving (Amiga-S) writes only the plain text; tabs are kept.

@{b}Agent@{ub}: here you enter tasks and see what the agent does: thinking steps, tool calls (with a diff for changes) and its answers. A / in the input opens the list of @{"commands" link COMMANDS}.

@{b}Mode@{ub}: sets how often the agent asks, see @{"Approvals and safety" link SAFETY}.

@{b}Build log@{ub}: the output of every command, whether from the agent or from you. Errors and warnings are bold; a double-click on an error message opens the file at the right line. In the line below you can enter commands yourself. It works like a small Shell: @{i}CD directory@{ui} changes the directory for the following commands (the left side shows where you are; CD without argument shows the full path), cursor up/down brings back earlier commands. The agent always works in the project directory, independent of this.

@{b}Status line@{ub}: IDE messages on the left, the token count on the right. @{i}Context@{ui} is the size of the last request relative to the configured context of the provider (see @{"Settings" link PREFS}); from 70% on the display turns bold, then the agent summarizes the session automatically. The in/out numbers count all tokens since the agent started (in = sent, out = answers), with providers that have a prompt cache also the cached part. With OpenAI and OpenRouter tokens cost money. When the provider reports the costs (OpenRouter), the amount in dollars since the agent started is shown bold at the far right.

On narrow screens (less than 800 pixels wide) agent, editor, files and build are stacked as register pages.

@endnode

@node AGENT "Working with the agent"
@{b}Working with the agent@{ub}

@{b}Good tasks@{ub}

Describe the goal, not every step. Name what matters: inputs, outputs, limits. Examples:

@{code}
  Write a Shell program that lists all files of a directory
  with their size and prints the total at the end. The
  directory comes as an argument (ReadArgs).

  Build the project and fix all compiler errors.

  The program crashes when the file is empty.
  Find the bug and fix it.

  Add an option QUIET that suppresses all output.

  Search the internet for how to open a file requester
  with asl.library, and build it in.
@{body}

@{b}What happens in the background@{ub}

The agent works in steps: it thinks, calls a tool (read file, change it, run a command ...), sees the result and continues until the task is done. After at most 30 steps it stops; with another task or "go on" it continues.

@{b}Rules for a project@{ub}

Everything the agent should always observe belongs in the file AMICODE.md in the project, for example "comments in English", "no floating point" or "only functions from OS 3.1". See @{"Project files" link PROJECTFILES}.

@{b}Abort@{ub}: the Stop button or CTRL-C aborts the running command or task.

@{b}Long sessions@{ub}: the conversation grows longer (and more expensive) with every step. @{"/compact" link COMMANDS} summarizes it, @{"/reset" link COMMANDS} starts anew.

@endnode

@node SESSIONS "Sessions"
@{b}Sessions@{ub}

A session is the conversation with the agent including all tool results. It is saved continuously in amicode.session in the project.

@{b}Nothing is lost@{ub}: when you start a new session (@{i}New session@{ui}, /reset, or the first task after the start), the old session moves to the archive .amicode/sessions/. The file name is the time of its last change.

@{b}Sessions window@{ub} (menu Agent, Amiga-J): the current session at the top, the archived ones below, newest first, each with date, size and the first task as title.

   @{i}Resume@{ui} (or double-click) - load the session; the current one is archived first. The last tasks and answers appear in the agent area, then just keep typing.
   @{i}Delete@{ui} - remove the archived session for good after a confirmation.

@{b}Resume last session@{ub} (menu Agent) loads amicode.session, for example after a crash.

The same exists as commands in the input line and in the Shell: /sessions, /resume no and /sessions delete no.

@endnode

@node SKILLS "Skills"
@{b}Skills - Amiga knowledge for the model@{ub} (menu Project / Skills..., Amiga-K)

Large models like Claude or GPT know the Amiga quite well. Local models like qwen have learned hardly anything about it: they invent Unix commands that do not exist or puzzle over how to open a library. Skills are short knowledge files that explain what is needed to the model.

@{b}Included@{ub}

@{code}
 amigados   AmigaDOS commands, paths, patterns, scripts
            (instead of Unix: List, Copy, Search, Execute ...)
 c-amiga    C on AmigaOS 3.x: libraries, types, files,
            windows, signals, compilers
 mui        MUI 3.8: objects, notifies, main loop
@{body}

@{b}Mode per skill@{ub}

   @{i}Always@{ui} - the content is a fixed part of the request to the model. Right for local models that do not reliably fetch knowledge themselves. Costs about 1000 tokens of context per skill.
   @{i}Automatic@{ui} (default) - the model only sees title and description and loads the skill when needed with the tool read_skill. Saves tokens with large models.
   @{i}Off@{ui} - the skill is not offered.

Choose a skill in the window, set the mode with the buttons and press @{i}Save@{ui}. The agent then restarts with the new knowledge; the current conversation continues. In the input line /skills shows the state.

@{b}Own skills@{ub}

A skill is a text file name.md in the drawer Skills next to AmiCodeIDE (for all projects) or in .amicode/skills/ in the project (only there, takes precedence). Structure:

@{code}
 # Title
 Description: One sentence on when the model needs the skill.

 Content: short rules and examples, preferably in English.
@{body}

Keep skills short (a few KB at most): everything in them goes to the model with every step.

@endnode

@node COMMANDS "Commands"
@{b}Commands@{ub} (in the agent input; a / shows the list)

@{code}
 /help           all commands
 /status         version, provider, model, mode, project
 /context        size of the conversation
 /tokens         tokens used in this session
 /model          list available models and switch for this
                 session (/model name)
 /compact [focus] summarize the conversation and continue
                 with that (saves tokens)
 /prune          remove old tool output
 /diff           show all changes of this session
 /undo           restore the file changed last
 /reset          start a new session (the old one is archived)
 /sessions       show earlier sessions
                 (/sessions delete no deletes one)
 /resume [no]    resume the last or an archived session
 /skills         Amiga knowledge (skills) and their mode
 /new            open the project assistant
 /toolchains     show the compilers found
                 (/toolchains scan searches again)
 !command        run a Shell command yourself; the output
                 goes to the agent with the next task
 !!command       run a Shell command just for yourself
@{body}

In the list you choose with the cursor keys and take one with Return. After /model the available models appear; typing on filters, for example /model claude.

@endnode

@node SAFETY "Approvals and safety"
@{b}Approvals and safety@{ub}

The Amiga has no memory protection and no recycle bin. That is why AmiCodeIDE watches closely what the agent may do.

@{b}Modes@{ub} (choice at the top right)

@{code}
 Safe     asks before every change, every command and every
          internet access
 Project  changes in the project and known commands (compilers,
          make, Execute of scripts, List ...) without asking;
          everything else is asked  (recommended)
 Full     everything in the project without asking
@{body}

@{b}The approval request@{ub} shows the full path and, for changes, the diff. @{i}Allow@{ui} applies once, @{i}Always@{ui} to all further calls of this tool in the session, @{i}Stop task@{ui} ends the task, @{i}Refuse@{ui} (also Esc) declines.

@{b}Always asked - in every mode@{ub}

- writing outside the project directory
- writing to SYS:, C:, S:, LIBS:, DEVS: and L:
- Format, Install, Relabel, Assign, Mount, AddBuffers
- Delete ... ALL
- starting self-built programs that are not in the list of allowed commands

@{b}Staleness protection@{ub}: the agent may only change a file if it has read it in this session and it has not changed since. So it never overwrites what you have edited in the editor in the meantime.

@{b}Backups and undo@{ub}: before the first change of a file AmiCodeIDE makes a copy in .amicode/backups/. @{i}Undo@{ui} (or /undo) restores the state before the first change of this session, @{i}Diff@{ui} (or /diff) shows all changes.

@{b}Log@{ub}: every action of the agent is recorded with the time in .amicode/audit.log - written before it is carried out, so that what happened can still be traced after a crash.

@{b}Crashes@{ub}: a faulty test program can halt the Amiga. Save open files before running. After a reboot, Resume last session (menu Agent) continues the conversation.

@endnode

@node TOOLCHAINS "Languages and tools"
@{b}Languages and tools@{ub}

AmiCodeIDE finds installed compilers and interpreters by itself (on all volumes in Developer/, Dev/, Programming/ or directly in the root). /toolchains shows them, /toolchains scan searches again.

@{code}
 vbcc       C with VBCC (+ NDK 3.2)
 sasc       C with SAS/C 6
 gcc        C with gcc 2.95 (ADE), without ixemul
 amiblitz   BASIC with AmiBlitz3 (command line compiler)
 purebasic  BASIC with PureBasic 4
 amigae     E with EC 3.3
 vasm       68000 assembler with vasm
 lua        Lua 5.0
 python     Python 2.0
 arexx      ARexx
 custom     own project, the agent chooses
@{body}

Every project gets a build script that sets the assigns and paths of its compiler on every run - so compilers do not get in each other's way.

@{b}Peculiarities@{ub} that the agent knows from AMICODE.md:

- AmiBlitz3 returns code 0 even on errors; whether the build worked is in the output ("0 errors").
- gcc is always used with -noixemul.
- PureBasic 4.00 needs no OpenConsole().
- E and PureBasic do not name a file with errors; the double-click in the build log then jumps into the main file.

AMOS Pro is not supported: source and compiler exist only in binary form there.

@endnode

@node WEB "Internet access"
@{b}Internet access@{ub}

The agent can search the internet (web_search, via DuckDuckGo) and read web pages as text (fetch_url). This way it looks up documentation, autodocs, Aminet descriptions or error messages by itself. This works with every provider, including Ollama.

- In the mode Safe every access asks first.
- With web=no in the section [agent] of the configuration, internet access is off completely.
- Every search and every fetch is recorded in the log.

Web pages are third-party text. The agent is instructed never to follow instructions from web pages and never to put private data into addresses or search queries. Pages that build their content only with JavaScript stay empty.

@endnode

@node CLI "Command line amicode"
@{b}The agent in the Shell: amicode@{ub}

amicode is the same agent without a user interface. It works in the current directory.

@{code}
 amicode [MODE safe|project|full] [STEPS n] [CONFIG file]
         [RESUME] [task]

 CD Work:Projects/myprog
 amicode Build the project and fix all errors
 amicode                      interactive, /help lists the commands
 amicode RESUME               resume an interrupted session
 amicode /sessions            show earlier sessions
 amicode "/resume 2"          load session no. 2
 amicode "/new vbcc Work:Projects/new"
@{body}

Answer questions with y (yes), n (no), a (always) or q (abort the task); Return means no. With the German catalog the letters are j, n, i and q. CTRL-C aborts the running command.

@endnode

@node CONFIG "Configuration"
@{b}Configuration@{ub} (ENVARC:AmiCode/amicode.conf)

Most values are set conveniently in the @{"settings window" link PREFS}. All keys:

@{code}
 [provider]
 active=openai        openai, openrouter, ollama, ollamacloud, custom
 stream=yes           show answers live

 [openai] / [openrouter] / [ollama] / [ollamacloud] / [custom]
 base=...             base URL, e.g. https://api.openai.com/v1
 api_key=...          API key (empty with Ollama)
 model=...            model
 context=32768        context in tokens (0 = limit of the provider);
                      summarized automatically from 70% on
 effort=low           reasoning effort: low, medium, high, default
                      (OpenRouter: default low, otherwise default)
 [ollama] in addition:
 think=yes|no         "thinking" of models like qwen3 on/off
 api=native|openai    interface (native knows the context size)

 [agent]
 mode=project         safe, project, full
 max_steps=30         steps per task
 cmd_timeout=600      seconds per command
 stack=200000         stack for commands
 allow=...            commands without asking in mode project
 web=yes              internet access
 max_context=60000    from this size (bytes) trim old output
                      (then to half, so that the cache holds)
 max_cost=2.00        cost limit per task in dollars (0 = none)

 [skills]
 amigados=auto        always, auto or off (per skill)
 session=amicode.session  session file (none = none)

 [theme]              colors and fonts (tab Appearance)
 light.editor.bg=...  MUI pen spec, e.g. rFFFFFFFF,FFFFFFFF,FFFFFFFF
 dark.editor.fg=...   (areas: editor, output, log, list, input,
                      status, window, button; .bg / .fg)
 dark.keyword=...     keyword, comment, string, preproc, cursor, mark
 font_fixed=XEN/11    fixed-width font (empty = MUI default)
 font_text=           font for agent, lists, input

 [ui]
 layout=auto          auto, narrow (register pages), wide
 projects=...         projects drawer (default: Projects next to the program)
 build=... / run=...  build/run, if not set in the project
@{body}

The compilers found are in ENVARC:AmiCode/toolchains.conf. The language is chosen with the variable AmiCodeLanguage, see @{"Language" link LANGUAGE}.

@endnode

@node PROJECTFILES "Project files"
@{b}Project files@{ub}

@{b}AMICODE.md@{ub} - rules and knowledge for the agent. It reads the file with every task. The project assistant writes tool, build command, target system and task into it; just add your own rules below.

@{b}build@{ub} - AmigaDOS script that builds the program (Execute build).

@{b}.amicode/settings@{ub} - project settings for the toolbar:
@{code}
 [ui]
 build=Execute build   command for the Build button
 run=myprog            command for the Run button
 main=myprog.c         main file (for error jumps)
@{body}

@{b}.amicode/backups/@{ub} - backups before changes by the agent.
@{b}.amicode/audit.log@{ub} - log of all actions.
@{b}amicode.session@{ub} - the current conversation (for "Resume session").
@{b}.amicode/sessions/@{ub} - earlier sessions, see @{"Sessions" link SESSIONS}.

@endnode

@node KEYS "Keyboard shortcuts"
@{b}Keyboard shortcuts@{ub}

@{code}
 Amiga-N   New project (assistant)
 Amiga-O   Open project
 Amiga-R   Reload file list
 Amiga-S   Save file
 Amiga-W   Close file
 Amiga-L   Clear build log
 Amiga-E   Settings
 Amiga-K   Skills
 Amiga-H   Manual
 Amiga-D   Show changes (diff)
 Amiga-Z   Undo last change
 Amiga-J   Sessions
 Amiga-.   Stop
 Amiga-?   About AmiCodeIDE
 Amiga-Q   Quit
 HELP      Manual for the current window
 Return    Send task / take command
 Cursor up/down  in the command line: earlier commands
@{body}

@endnode

@node TROUBLE "Tips and troubleshooting"
@{b}Tips and troubleshooting@{ub}

@{b}"TLS: certificate rejected"@{ub} - usually the system clock is wrong. Set the clock (preferably via NTP, for example with AmiTimeKeeper) and try again.

@{b}"No API key" / "HTTP 401"@{ub} - check the key in the @{"settings window" link PREFS}.

@{b}Ollama does not answer@{ub} - is the Ollama server running and reachable in the network (OLLAMA_HOST=0.0.0.0)? The base URL ends in :11434/v1 and starts with http:// (not https).

@{b}Local model forgets the beginning or goes round in circles@{ub} - the context is too small for the task. In the @{"settings window" link PREFS} under Ollama enter a larger value for @{i}Context (tokens)@{ui} (32768 is a good value if the graphics card has enough memory). AmiCodeIDE summarizes the session automatically from 70 percent on; /compact also helps in between.

@{b}Ollama reports "out of memory" or becomes very slow@{ub} - the context is too large for the graphics card. Enter a smaller value for @{i}Context (tokens)@{ui}, for example 16384.

@{b}The agent goes round in circles@{ub} - press Stop, describe the task more precisely or split it into parts, choose a stronger model.

@{b}Build does not find headers@{ub} - the build script sets the assigns; check whether the compiler is still in the place found (/toolchains scan).

@{b}The user interface does not open@{ub} - is a MUI class missing? See @{"Requirements" link REQUIRE}.

@{b}Keeping an eye on costs@{ub} - with every step the whole conversation goes to the model again; long sessions therefore get expensive. With OpenRouter the status line shows the costs in dollars, and the line after every task the costs of that task; the @{"cost limit" link PREFS} stops a task before it gets too expensive. The biggest savings come from a clear task and an AMICODE.md with the build recipe: an agent that first has to search headers uses many times as much. /tokens shows the usage, /compact summarizes long sessions, /reset starts anew. For Claude and Gemini via OpenRouter AmiCodeIDE uses prompt caching: the unchanged beginning is then charged at only about a tenth (the line after every task shows "... of them cached"). With Claude Haiku the cache only kicks in for longer requests. OpenAI caches automatically.

@endnode

@node LIMITS "Limits"
@{b}Limits@{ub}

- The agent sees no windows: with GUI programs it does not know what the result looks like. Describe to it what you see.
- There is no debugger; after a crash, the guru number you tell the agent helps.
- A faulty test program can halt the Amiga (no memory protection).
- The agent cannot read web pages that only work with JavaScript.
- AMOS Pro is not supported.

@endnode

